#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514} :activity/id #uuid "34909092-851c-5b8f-b289-94633fbc239b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/id #uuid "3afe28a0-5051-52a1-a085-a06727c301b8" :topic/math-academy-id 1047} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097840 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z=1 \\\\ y-z=2 \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix for this system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is in *row echelon form* (or REF). Whenever the augmented matrix is in row echelon form, we can solve the system using a method called **back substitution**.\n\nBack substitution involves\n\n- starting with the lowest equation (which is already solved for one variable),\n\n- substituting the result into the next-lowest equation to find the solution for another variable, and\n\n- continuing to substitute the result into the next-lowest equation until the values of all the variables have been found.\n\nHere, the third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny -(3) = 2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $y=5$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx + 2(5) + (3) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = -12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3.$ We can write it in vector form, as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The values $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3$ together form *one* solution. We say that this system has *one* solution, *not* three solutions. A system with three solutions would have three different solution vectors, whereas here, we only have *one* solution vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d84a9a0-94f9-57e2-a8e6-333ba26d72e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b94c8727-6dff-5c9a-9868-815a8a185978" :step/math-academy-id 17179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265732}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "47cad0ff-3b21-5238-b205-26619a15e0b5" :step/math-academy-id 17180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341890}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4f928835-23b6-530a-b253-30